# {{DISPLAY_NAME}} — iPhone app

This is a complete Xcode project for your app. Everything you set in your Servbiz
dashboard is already configured: the name, icon, colours, screen behaviour and
permissions. You do not need to write any code.

## Put it on your own iPhone

You need a Mac with **Xcode** (free from the Mac App Store) and a free Apple ID.

1. Unzip this folder.
2. Open **{{PRODUCT_NAME}}.xcodeproj**.
3. Click the project name in the left sidebar, then the **Signing & Capabilities**
   tab. Under *Team*, choose your Apple ID. Add one with *Add an Account* if the
   list is empty.
4. Plug in your iPhone and pick it from the device menu at the top of the window.
5. Press **Run** (the play button).

The first time, your iPhone will refuse to open the app until you trust the
developer certificate: on the phone, go to **Settings → General → VPN & Device
Management**, tap your Apple ID, then **Trust**.

An app signed with a free Apple ID stops working after **7 days** and has to be
re-run from Xcode. That is Apple's limit for free accounts, not ours.

## Put it on the App Store

That needs the **Apple Developer Program** (99 USD a year, paid to Apple). Once
you have it, the easiest path is to go back to your Servbiz dashboard, add your
App Store Connect key, and choose **Signed app** — we will build the finished,
signed file for you and you upload it.

If you would rather do it here: set your paid team under *Signing &
Capabilities*, then **Product → Archive**, then **Distribute App**.

## What you are looking at

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `Sources/Config.swift` | Your settings, as code. Regenerated on every build from the dashboard. |
| `Sources/WebViewController.swift` | The screen that shows your site. |
| `Sources/AppDelegate.swift` | App startup. |
| `Resources/Info.plist` | Name, version, permission prompts. |
| `Resources/Assets.xcassets` | Your icon and launch colour. |

It is yours to change. If you edit it and later build again from the dashboard,
you will get a fresh copy and your edits will not be in it — so keep your own
version somewhere if you start making changes.

## Things worth knowing

**It only opens your site.** The app refuses to load any other website. That is
deliberate: it carries your name and your customers' logged-in sessions.

**Permissions are fixed at build time.** If your site starts needing the camera,
switch it on in the dashboard and build again — it cannot be granted from inside
the app.

**Apple reviews the experience, not just the code.** An app that is only a website
in a frame is sometimes rejected under App Store guideline 4.2 ("minimum
functionality"). Apps with real features behind a login — ordering, booking,
accounts — generally pass. Worth knowing before you pay for the membership.

